  "code": "use IO.Std.write_line;\n\n// Knuth's A, with the by-name arguments x1 to x5 written as functions, which is what call by name\n// amounts to here. Each call to A gets its own k, and the B it builds captures that one: a `let\n// mut` is captured by reference, so B decrements the k of the call it was made in, even when it\n// is called long afterwards from somewhere further down.\na(k: int, x1: () -> int, x2: () -> int, x3: () -> int, x4: () -> int, x5: () -> int) -> int is\n    let counter mut = k;\n\n    let b = () rec =>\n        val\n            counter = counter - 1;\n\n            a(counter, rec, x1, x2, x3, x4)\n        lav;\n\n    return if counter <= 0 then x4() + x5() else b() fi;\nsi\n\nfor k in 0::10 do\n    write_line(\"k = {k,2}: {a(k, () => 1, () => -1, () => -1, () => 1, () => 0)}\");\nod",
